gm-method (a user-reported visual bug that the headless browser witness CANNOT reproduce, after exhaustive measurement): when every instrument says the thing is correct -- full geometry, zero degenerate/origin vertices in the decoded buffer, all draws stable across real animate-loop frames, identical code served on the 'good' and 'bad' surfaces -- but the user still sees the bug, STOP escalating blind fixes. Two real gaps a headless harness genuinely cannot cross: (1) the user's REAL GPU may exhibit driver/precision/depth behavior the headless ANGLE/SwiftShader GPU does not (a NaN-collapse or z-fight that only fires on their hardware); (2) INTERACTIVE state the harness cannot enter -- a player avatar that only spawns after a real pointer-lock click-to-play, so its mesh/bones are uninspectable headlessly. In both cases the honest move is to declare blockedBy:external and ASK THE USER for the one witness that localizes it (a screenshot of the actual failure, when-does-it-happen conditions that discriminate the candidate code paths, their GPU string) -- NOT to keep editing a sibling library on an unconfirmed hypothesis. Editing shared/library code blind to suppress a symptom you cannot reproduce is exactly how a 'fix' becomes a regression (a guard that over-culls, a cull-disable that masks nothing). A clarifying question that discriminates between 'far/zoom' (distance cull), 'screen-edge/rotate' (frustum cull), and 'always/up-close' (decode or geometry) collapses the hypothesis space far faster than another headless render. Use AskUserQuestion with options mapped to distinct code paths.